---
// The stable destination for every "get the app" link on this site and off it.
//
// It exists so that prose — blog posts, a TikTok bio, a Reddit comment — never
// has to contain a play.google.com URL. Markdown cannot read PLAY_STORE_URL, so
// a hard-coded store link in a post goes stale silently; two of them already did,
// pointing at a listing that was not public yet. This page is the indirection.
//
// It is indexable on purpose: "cirrus quit vaping app download" is a brand
// navigational query and this is the page that should answer it.
//
// IT IS ALSO WHERE /get GIVES UP, which is a second job and a load-bearing one.
// `functions/get.ts` sends Android to Play and iPhone to the App Store off the
// User-Agent, and lands everyone it cannot place here — desktop, crawlers, and
// the two device classes no header can identify: an iPad in Safari (a desktop
// Mac UA since iPadOS 13) and a large Android tablet in Chrome's default
// desktop mode (an X11 Linux UA). Both of those are real people on real phones
// and tablets, so this page must always show EVERY store that is open, never
// assume the reader is on the platform the redirect failed to detect, and never
// become a stub that forwards back to /get.
//
// ONE QUERY, ONE PAGE: the home FAQ owns "Is Cirrus free?" and "When is Cirrus
// available, and is it on iPhone?". This page summarises both in the visitor's
// path and links home rather than re-answering them, and it deliberately emits
// no FAQPage schema of its own.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import WaitlistForm from '../components/WaitlistForm.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE, LAUNCH_PLATFORM } from '../consts';
import { STORE_LIVE, IOS_LIVE, playUrl, appStoreUrl } from '../lib/store';

// Both stores open is the only state where nobody needs a waitlist, so it is
// the only state where the form comes off the page.
const anyStore = STORE_LIVE || IOS_LIVE;

// The meta description is the one line Google shows under the result, so it
// cannot keep saying "iPhone to follow" the week iPhone stops following.
const metaDescription = IOS_LIVE
  ? 'How to get Cirrus, the quit vaping app that counts your real puffs and tapers them to zero. On Android and iPhone, with a free tier that keeps working.'
  : 'How to get Cirrus, the quit vaping app that counts your real puffs and tapers them to zero. Android first, iPhone to follow, free tier that keeps working.';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Get Cirrus"
  description={metaDescription}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Get the app' }]}
>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <h1>Get {SITE_TITLE}</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      A puff counter that tapers you down instead of demanding you stop dead. One bad Tuesday
      shouldn't cost you three weeks.
    </p>

    {
      anyStore && (
        <p class="stores">
          {/* TODO: swap for the official Google Play and App Store badge SVGs once
              the assets are in public/. Text buttons are compliant in the meantime;
              a recoloured or redrawn badge would not be. */}
          {STORE_LIVE && (
            <a class="btn btn-primary" href={playUrl('download-page')} rel="noopener">
              Get it free on Google Play
            </a>
          )}
          {IOS_LIVE && (
            <a class="btn btn-white" href={appStoreUrl('download-page')} rel="noopener">
              Get it free on the App Store
            </a>
          )}
        </p>
      )
    }

    {
      /* The waitlist does not retire when Android ships — it becomes the iPhone
         list. Roughly half of this site's readers are on iOS, and sending them
         to an Android-only button with no alternative is how you lose the half
         of the audience you already paid to reach. It comes off the page only
         when their store is actually open. */
      !IOS_LIVE &&
        (STORE_LIVE ? (
          <>
            <p>
              On iPhone? It's built and it's with Apple — we'll email you the day it clears
              review. That's the only thing we'll ever send you.
            </p>
            <WaitlistForm
              source="download-ios"
              id="download-ios-waitlist"
              label="On iPhone"
              cta="Tell me when iOS lands"
            />
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            <p>
              It isn't on the store yet — and we'd rather say that than take a pre-order for
              something you can't open. Leave your email and you'll hear the day it lands on your
              platform. That's the only thing we'll ever send you.
            </p>
            <WaitlistForm source="download" id="download-waitlist" cta="Tell me when it's out" />
          </>
        ))
    }

    <h2>What you get</h2>
    <p>
      The free tier keeps working forever: puff logging, streaks, money saved, your daily limit,
      the community, and a few coach messages a day. Paid unlocks the adaptive plan and unlimited
      coaching. There's no lockout, we never sell your data, and there are no ad trackers in the
      app — we removed the advertising ID rather than switching it off.
    </p>

    <h2>Which phone</h2>
    {
      IOS_LIVE ? (
        <p>
          Both. Android and iPhone run the same app, the same plan and the same account — sign in
          on either and your history follows you.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          {LAUNCH_PLATFORM} first. The iPhone build exists and it's with Apple for review, so the
          waitlist is the right move if that's you — it's the same list, and you'll get one email
          when your version is ready.
        </p>
      )
    }

    <p>
      Not sure yet? <a href="/">See what a year of vaping is costing you</a> — it takes about ten
      seconds and it needs no signup.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Two buttons sit side by side on a desktop and stack on a phone. `.btn` is
     inline-flex, so a plain <p> would leave them jammed against each other with
     only a text space between; this is a flex row with a real gap that wraps
     rather than overflowing. */
  .stores {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
